WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:02.129
Film Courage: What’s the fastest way to
learn screenwriting?

00:00:02.129 --> 00:00:06.000
Matthew Kalil, Author/Screenwriter/Director:
The fastest way to learn screenwriting is

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.410
very easy…read scripts.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.280
It’s that simple.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.820
So people who write novels very often just
sit down at their computer and start writing.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.500
But chances are they’ve read lots of novels
in their lives.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.500
Almost certainly.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.980
So there’s this famous saying if you want
to know how to write to read, read, read and

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.720
write, write, write.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.779
Script writing is exactly the same, read lots
of scripts.

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:34.559
What I find really strange is participants
at my workshops and people who come to my

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:38.390
talks and stuff, I’m like “How many of
you have read a feature film script?”

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:43.050
And like out of the 40 people (maybe it’s
a South African thing) not many people have

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.180
read a feature film script but they want to
write movies because they’ve watched lots

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.180
of movies.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.190
So they think because they are a movie fan
and they like to watch movies they can write

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:55.530
scripts and I’m like “But you’ve never
seen this document of a script properly?”

00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:00.620
You may have read transcripts of the script
but you’ve never looked at the formatting

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.620
and the layout and the style and the structure
and all of this kind of thing.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.399
So I would say just read as many scripts as
you can and they’re all available online

00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:10.319
(there are websites where you can get them).

00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:14.890
Because I think that’s how we learn is just
from reading.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.560
And if you read many, many feature film scripts
then you’ll end up writing feature film

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.560
scripts.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.159
For me that’s the fast track to writing
good scripts is reading lots of scripts and

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:25.030
they’re there, it’s available.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:28.539
You can read the screenwriting books on how
to write scripts but really if you just read

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:32.999
scripts (lots of them) and keep up-to-date
with what’s happening and the trends of

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:36.100
writing styles and how things are changing
through the years…you know don’t just

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.729
read CASABLANCA and CHINATOWN because they
are certain formatting at a certain time.

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:40.880
Read current scripts as well.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.719
If you’re interested in TV series, read
TV series scripts because they’re also written

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:47.670
in a different style and then you start to
see the writer’s voice and then perhaps

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.849
from that you can start writing in your own
voice.

00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:53.310
So I would say the fast track is just read
loads of scripts.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.880
Then of course, write loads of scripts.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.139
My first scripts that I ever wrote does not
exist anymore anyway in this world (I hope).

00:02:01.139 --> 00:02:02.679
There is no digital copy of it.

00:02:02.679 --> 00:02:03.679
It was terrible.

00:02:03.679 --> 00:02:05.529
It probably had a lot of formatting errors.

00:02:05.529 --> 00:02:11.450
It wasn’t terrible it was just misguided,
the enthusiasm of youth.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:15.040
So you’ve got to write quite a lot to get
that stuff out of your system I think or you’ll

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.050
get the genius writer who may write the first
scripts and it’s like fantastic but I’d

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.230
say write a few times and see how it works.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:27.909
Get feedback as well from people who are not
your mother or your best friend and then rewrite

00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:30.060
that script and then you’ll start to get
somewhere.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.239
Yes, I suppose that’s the fast track to
writing.

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:40.599
It goes without saying one needs to watch
lots of movies and be steeped in that world.

00:02:40.599 --> 00:02:48.260
And then to make it really good.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.020
I was going to say read the book The Three
Wells of Screenwriting…

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.620
Film Courage: And that’s your book?

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:58.109
Matthew: Yes…but what I want to say is tap
into what only you can bring into the script.

00:02:58.109 --> 00:03:01.659
So it’s all very well if you read loads
of scripts and writing loads of scripts if

00:03:01.659 --> 00:03:07.069
you’re just going to write another crime
thriller or…something that someone else

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:08.249
can do better.

00:03:08.249 --> 00:03:10.590
What can you write?

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.890
The story that only you know, the stories
that only you can write, the themes that only

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:14.890
you can write.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:21.730
If you can tap into that then you are really
putting something in there that is authentic

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:25.000
and people will recognize it when they read
it immediately.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.659
So I added a lot of script reading in the
past and for competitions and I’ve read

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:31.969
too many scripts…yeah…for my own sanity.

00:03:31.969 --> 00:03:37.969
But when you’re reading them, suddenly something
leaps off of the page and that’s authenticity,

00:03:37.969 --> 00:03:43.310
there’s a moment of realness, of real character
moment, a moment of subtlety, a real location,

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.019
a moment of something that leaps off of the
page.

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:49.670
If you can put that in your scripts then I
really believe you’ll have an advantage

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:50.819
over other scripts.

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:54.319
So yes, read lots of scripts and write the
kind of scripts that you can write.

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:56.920
Film Courage: And what was the first script
that you read?

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:00.719
Matthew: Oh, that’s another great question?

00:04:00.719 --> 00:04:04.359
Film Courage: Do you remember the year?

00:04:04.359 --> 00:04:10.299
Matthew: It was probably quite late, well
I’ve read plays.

00:04:10.299 --> 00:04:14.590
The first thing I’ve read were plays (stage
plays) before I read scripts.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.530
Things like Waiting For Godot.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:21.039
You know the standard plays you read when
getting an English degree or in South Africa

00:04:21.039 --> 00:04:24.560
we read some plays.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:35.090
And the first scripts I read was probably
CINEMA PARADISO and it’s a movie by an Italian

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:42.210
filmmaker Giuseppe Tornatore and he won the…many,
many years ago he wrote and shot the beautiful

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:46.050
movie CINEMA PARADISO and I love that movie.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:50.500
I think that was the first script I ever bought
and the first script I ever read.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.870
But just the caveat goes it was one of those
transcribed scripts that you can buy so it’s

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:58.110
like a book version of a script and it’s
got all of the dialogue and stuff in it and

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:02.030
some of the action but it wasn’t formatted
in a correct script format.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:06.410
I think the first one I read was probably
CHINATOWN in proper script formatting.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:10.810
But I remember buying the book of CINEMA PARADISO
and reading it and being able to picture all

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:15.039
of the scenes in my head and that’s a real
skill that people kind of have to develop.

00:05:15.039 --> 00:05:19.360
You’ve got to read these scripts to picture
in your head, to feel what it feels like so

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.530
when you write you can make someone picture
those scenes in their head.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.979
It’s a different thing.

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:30.199
I often say that for me when people think
they can write scripts without reading scripts

00:05:30.199 --> 00:05:34.379
it’s like going to a classical concert and
listening to a piece of classical music and

00:05:34.379 --> 00:05:35.860
thinking “Oh I love this music.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:37.910
I’m going to go home and write some music
now.”

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.669
And just writing music randomly with dots
and squiggles because you know there’s dots

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:45.569
and squiggles and lines and just sort of writing
some type of document that you think looks

00:05:45.569 --> 00:05:48.819
like sheet music and then handing it over
to someone and expecting them to play?

00:05:48.819 --> 00:05:50.419
They’re not going to understand.

00:05:50.419 --> 00:05:54.050
It’s like an architect, if you see a beautiful
building and you think “Wow, this building

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:55.050
is really beautiful.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.050
I love this building.”

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.440
I like buildings and I understand buildings
and I like Art Deco style.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.539
I’m going to go home and design a building
and you just kind of design the building without

00:06:02.539 --> 00:06:08.389
having looked a blueprints or read sheet music,
that’s why you’ve got to read scripts.

00:06:08.389 --> 00:06:09.389
A movie is not a script.

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:10.389
It’s a different thing.

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:12.690
They serve different kind of functions almost,
so yeah.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:17.199
Film Courage: You can’t be Frank Gehry right
away unless you study.

00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:18.199
Matthew: Right.

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:20.639
Film Courage: And the same thing, you can’t
be William Goldman if you don’t study.

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:21.639
Matthew: Right.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:23.130
William Goldman just did whatever he wanted
to really.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:29.180
But I think it’s about being able to evoke
an emotion in the reader.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.170
So maybe they can do that.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:36.250
They really had a way of doing it some how
in their writing.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:43.270
Film Courage: Well I think people think of
the notion of genius or savant and think “Maybe

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:48.169
I can duplicate the same without having to
take all of the steps in between.”

00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:56.560
And we hear these stories of people from age
5 being able to do something amazing.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.990
Maybe it is inside of us but needs a little
coaching?

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:02.560
Matthew: I think screenwriting is a bit of
a craft.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.540
So you don’t actually hear of savant screenwriters.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:09.479
Like I’ve never heard of a five-year-old
writing a great script or even a 17-year-old

00:07:09.479 --> 00:07:10.510
writing a great script.

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:15.919
Sometimes you can get…you know the woman
who wrote JUNO [Diablo Cody] she was relatively

00:07:15.919 --> 00:07:17.370
young when she wrote that.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:21.580
But I don’t really know what happened there,
I know some of the story.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:28.650
But because it’s a craft, it’s like pottery
or something.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:34.030
If you’re a genius potter you still have
to practice to get the pot to be correct and

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:39.060
I really believe with screenwriting it is
a bit of a craft and so we don’t get these

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.449
savant we just don’t.

00:07:40.449 --> 00:07:44.190
We don’t get the 17-year-old fetch the best
screenwriting award.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.479
Not yet although that might change because
the youngsters nowadays and the access they

00:07:47.479 --> 00:07:50.990
have filmmaking ability is very exciting and
amazing.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:54.129
I mean they’ve got things on the phones
(apps) where they can just make music videos

00:07:54.129 --> 00:07:55.180
just like that.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:56.680
So maybe it will change.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:03.410
Maybe the ability to make what is in your
head won’t have this craft in-between step

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:06.150
and will have this youngsters making the best
films ever.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.150
Who knows?

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:08.430
It would be nice.

